<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>ColabHub Project Documentation</title><style>body{font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;line-height:1.45;color:#202124;margin:2.2cm}h1{color:#17365d;border-bottom:2px solid #2f75b5;padding-bottom:6px}h2{color:#1f4e79;margin-top:24px}h3{color:#2f5597}code,pre{font-family:Consolas,monospace;background:#f2f2f2;padding:2px 4px}pre{padding:10px;border-left:4px solid #2f75b5}li{margin:4px 0}.note{background:#eaf3f8;border-left:4px solid #2f75b5;padding:10px}</style></head><body>
<h1>ColabHub Project Documentation</h1>
<p><b>ColabHub</b> is a Chrome Manifest V3 extension that syncs Google Colab notebooks to GitHub automatically, similar to LeetHub.</p>
<p><b>Repository:</b> https://github.com/Subhadip-learner/colabhub</p>
<h2>1. Project goal</h2><p>The project removes repetitive manual notebook backup. Users connect GitHub, select a repository, work normally in Colab, and the extension commits notebook changes after saves or cell runs.</p>
<h2>2. Normal user flow</h2><ol><li>Install or load the extension in Chrome.</li><li>Open a Drive-backed notebook in Google Colab.</li><li>Click <b>Connect GitHub</b> and authorize with the GitHub Device Flow code.</li><li>Allow Google Drive access during the first sync.</li><li>Create a new repository or connect an existing repository.</li><li>Keep <b>Auto-Push</b> enabled and click <b>Connect &amp; Sync</b>.</li><li>Run cells in Colab; the notebook is committed to GitHub automatically.</li></ol>
<h2>3. Gmail and Google OAuth</h2><p>If the Google OAuth app is in <b>Testing</b> mode, only accounts listed under <b>Google Auth Platform -&gt; Audience -&gt; Test users</b> can authorize. Therefore, during testing, a friend/tester must send their Gmail to the developer so it can be added to the Test users list.</p><p>For a public release, move the app to <b>In production</b> and complete Google verification when required. Then public users do not need to send their Gmail or be manually added as test users.</p><div class="note"><b>Never ask users for passwords, GitHub tokens, Client Secrets, Cloudflare secrets, or Drive access tokens.</b></div>
<h2>4. Architecture</h2><ul><li><b>extension/</b>: Chrome extension runtime.</li><li><b>background.js</b>: authentication, Drive sync, GitHub commits, alarms, badge, and status.</li><li><b>content/colab.js</b>: detects Colab save/cell-run events and shows toasts.</li><li><b>popup/</b>: connection, repository, Auto-Push, Auto-Sync, and path controls.</li><li><b>lib/</b>: auth, Drive, GitHub API, notebook parsing, storage, hashing, and sync engine.</li><li><b>backend/worker.js</b>: optional secure OAuth token exchange; secrets stay server-side.</li><li><b>tests/</b>: automated behavior tests.</li></ul>
<h2>5. Cell run to GitHub</h2><ol><li>Content script detects a cell execution.</li><li>Background worker debounces events for about 8 seconds.</li><li>Drive metadata confirms the notebook was saved.</li><li>Notebook is normalized, hashed, and scanned for secrets.</li><li>Remote SHA is compared with the last synced SHA.</li><li>Conflicts are reported instead of silently overwritten.</li><li>GitHub Contents API creates or updates <code>notebooks/&lt;real-name&gt;.ipynb</code>.</li><li>Popup, toolbar badge, and Colab toast show the result.</li></ol>
<h2>6. Features</h2><ul><li>GitHub Device Flow login</li><li>Google Drive read-only notebook access</li><li>Auto-Push after cell run</li><li>Auto-Sync after save or interval</li><li>Whole notebook, Python script, or outputs-log granularity</li><li>Conflict detection</li><li>Secret scanning before commit</li><li>Correct Drive filename, for example <code>notebooks/ridge_regression.ipynb</code></li><li>Minified release packaging and server-side secrets</li></ul>
<h2>7. Security</h2><p>Client secrets are never shipped in the extension. The Worker stores the standard OAuth secret. Tokens stay in Chrome storage. Generated archives and secret files are ignored by Git. Browser extension source cannot be made completely uncopyable, so minification, licensing, and server-side secrets provide practical protection.</p>
<h2>8. Validation</h2><p>The project has 50 automated tests covering authentication, Worker security, notebook serialization, hashing, secret scanning, conflicts, Auto-Sync, Auto-Push, repository linking, and granularity conversion.</p><pre>npm test
npm run package</pre>
<h2>9. Recruiter summary</h2><p>This project demonstrates MV3 extension architecture, OAuth/device authentication, Google Drive and GitHub REST APIs, Cloudflare Workers, event-driven synchronization, debouncing, retry alarms, hash-based change detection, conflict-safe writes, secret scanning, automated testing, release packaging, and iterative UX design.</p>
<h2>10. Limitations</h2><ul><li>Chrome/Chromium browsers only.</li><li>Notebook must be saved in Google Drive.</li><li>Large notebook outputs may exceed GitHub Contents API limits.</li><li>Public Google release requires OAuth consent configuration and possibly verification.</li><li>Auto-Push waits for Colab to save the notebook before committing.</li></ul>
</body></html>